  "query_preview": "..\nThe monsoon surge that has brought strong to near gale winds to the \nMarianas over the last couple of days will persist tonight before \nbeginning a weakening trend over the next couple of days. Although \nwinds look to decrease towards the middle of the week, the monsoon \nlooks to remain over the Marianas, keeping showers chances medium to \nhigh-end scattered (40-50%). The Wind Advisory remains in effect \nuntil 6 AM Wednesday. 20 to 30 mph winds with gusts to 40 mph will \ncontinue to be possible, especially in and around heavier showers. \nWinds look to decrease to 10 to 15 mph by Wednesday night as a short \nlull in higher winds moves over the region. \n\nTowards the latter half of the week, into this weekend, and into \nnext week, the forecast is dependent on the location and strength of \nnewly designated Invest 96W located south of Wake Island. Invest 96W \nis currently weak with an elongated circulation, see the tropical \ndiscussion for more details. The westerly winds feeding into Invest \n96W will intensify as the pressure gradient grows stronger. This \nwill allow the monsoon to re-strengthen over the Marianas leading to \na secondary surge. Uncertainty remains high on the placement of the \nmonsoon surge. The GFS treats 96W more like a fully tropical system \nearly next week, leading to stronger winds closer to the circulation \nand north of the CNMI. Meanwhile, the ECMWF has more of a \nsubtropical appearance with the strong winds well south of the \ncirculation, over the Marianas. The impacts would differ greatly \nwith the potential for heavy rainfall and near gale winds near the \ncenter of the monsoon surge. A hydrological product may be needed in \nthe coming days if confidence increases in the southern solution. \nThose in the Marianas should continue to monitor forecasts for \nupdates in the progression of the monsoon.",
